From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Alessandro Ferri <aleferri@example.invalid>
Date: Wed, 3 Sep 2026 00:30:00 +0200
Subject: [PATCH 12/12] b43: write the shared-memory cells the AC cores expect

A set of shared-memory cells that b43 has been leaving at whatever the
microcode starts them with, and that the OEM driver writes during bring-up.
b43.h already names all of them.

    SLOTT        9        slot time
    MAXBFRAMES   8        maximum frames in a burst
    ANTSWAP      0x000a   antenna swap threshold
    BTSFOFF      0x003a   beacon TSF offset
    DEFAULTIV    0x000a   default IV location
    RFATT        0x0480   radio attenuation
    HOSTF4       0x0060   host flags 4
    HOSTF5       0x8088   host flags 5
    EDCFQ        0        EDCF queue info
    PSM          0        PSM transmitter address match block

Every value is the same on a BCM4352 and on a BCM4360, across every channel
and both driver versions the captures cover, so none of it is board data.

The slot time is the one value here that is derived rather than observed: 9 is
the 802.11a slot time, the only one that band has. b43_set_slot_time() had been
leaving the cell alone because writing it hurt the transmit rate on BCM4311 and
that was never understood, and returning before doing anything at all on 5 GHz
because the IFS slot register it writes is a 2.4 GHz affair. The cell is now
written from revision 42, which leaves BCM4311 and everything else as it was,
and 5 GHz gets it during core init rather than waiting for mac80211 to report
an ERP slot change that cannot come on a band with one slot time.

For the rest the meaning is not established beyond the name, and the values are
observed. They are written because the hardware is evidently expected to have
them: leaving a cell at a default the OEM driver always overwrites is a guess
too, and the worse one.

Where a cell is written more than once during bring-up -- host flags 4 and 5
gain bits as the driver goes, the beacon TSF offset changes once -- the value
here is the one it ends at. b43's core init has a different shape from the
OEM's and the intermediate points have no counterpart in it, so the end state
matches and the order does not.

Left alone: the probe response offload cells, PRSSID, PRSSIDLEN and PRTLEN.
b43 turns that offload off with PRMAXTIME, so they are meant to be untouched.

Signed-off-by: Alessandro Ferri <aleferri@example.invalid>
---
 drivers/net/wireless/broadcom/b43/main.c | 72 ++++++++++++++++++++++++++-----
 1 file changed, 64 insertions(+), 8 deletions(-)

--- a/drivers/net/wireless/broadcom/b43/main.c
+++ b/drivers/net/wireless/broadcom/b43/main.c
@@ -823,17 +823,23 @@
 static void b43_set_slot_time(struct b43_wldev *dev, u16 slot_time)
 {
 	/* slot_time is in usec. */
-	/* This test used to exit for all but a G PHY. */
+
+	/*
+	 * The shared-memory slot time was left alone here because writing it
+	 * hurt the transmit rate on BCM4311, and that was never understood. It
+	 * is still left alone on those cores. From revision 42 it is written,
+	 * and it is the only thing done on 5 GHz: there the OEM driver writes
+	 * 9 and nothing else, which is the 802.11a slot time, and the IFS slot
+	 * register below is a 2.4 GHz affair -- this function used to return
+	 * early on 5 GHz for that reason.
+	 */
+	if (dev->dev->core_rev >= 42)
+		b43_shm_write16(dev, B43_SHM_SHARED, B43_SHM_SH_SLOTT,
+				slot_time);
+
 	if (b43_current_band(dev->wl) == NL80211_BAND_5GHZ)
 		return;
 	b43_write16(dev, B43_MMIO_IFSSLOT, 510 + slot_time);
-	/* Shared memory location 0x0010 is the slot time and should be
-	 * set to slot_time; however, this register is initially 0 and changing
-	 * the value adversely affects the transmit rate for BCM4311
-	 * devices. Until this behavior is unterstood, delete this step
-	 *
-	 * b43_shm_write16(dev, B43_SHM_SHARED, 0x0010, slot_time);
-	 */
 }
 
 static void b43_short_slot_timing_enable(struct b43_wldev *dev)
@@ -5012,6 +5018,56 @@
 	b43_shm_write16(dev, B43_SHM_SHARED, B43_SHM_SH_SFFBLIM, 3);
 	b43_shm_write16(dev, B43_SHM_SHARED, B43_SHM_SH_LFFBLIM, 2);
 
+	/*
+	 * Cells b43 has been leaving at whatever the microcode starts them
+	 * with, and that the OEM driver writes. Every value below is the same
+	 * on a BCM4352 and on a BCM4360, across every channel and both driver
+	 * versions, so none of it is board data.
+	 *
+	 * The meaning of most of these is not established beyond the name
+	 * b43.h already carries, and the values are observed rather than
+	 * derived. They are written because the hardware is evidently expected
+	 * to have them: leaving a cell at a microcode default the OEM driver
+	 * always overwrites is a guess too, and the worse one.
+	 *
+	 * Where a cell is written more than once during bring-up -- host flags
+	 * 4 and 5 gain bits as the driver goes, beacon TSF offset changes once
+	 * -- the value here is the one it ends at. b43's core init has a
+	 * different shape from the OEM's and the intermediate points have no
+	 * counterpart in it, so the end state matches and the order does not.
+	 *
+	 * Not written: the probe response offload cells, PRSSID, PRSSIDLEN and
+	 * PRTLEN. b43 turns that offload off with PRMAXTIME just below, so
+	 * they are meant to be untouched.
+	 */
+	if (dev->dev->core_rev >= 42) {
+		b43_shm_write16(dev, B43_SHM_SHARED,
+				B43_SHM_SH_MAXBFRAMES, 8);
+		b43_shm_write16(dev, B43_SHM_SHARED,
+				B43_SHM_SH_ANTSWAP, 0x000a);
+		b43_shm_write16(dev, B43_SHM_SHARED,
+				B43_SHM_SH_BTSFOFF, 0x003a);
+		b43_shm_write16(dev, B43_SHM_SHARED,
+				B43_SHM_SH_DEFAULTIV, 0x000a);
+		b43_shm_write16(dev, B43_SHM_SHARED,
+				B43_SHM_SH_RFATT, 0x0480);
+		b43_shm_write16(dev, B43_SHM_SHARED,
+				B43_SHM_SH_HOSTF4, 0x0060);
+		b43_shm_write16(dev, B43_SHM_SHARED,
+				B43_SHM_SH_HOSTF5, 0x8088);
+		b43_shm_write16(dev, B43_SHM_SHARED, B43_SHM_SH_EDCFQ, 0);
+		b43_shm_write16(dev, B43_SHM_SHARED, B43_SHM_SH_PSM, 0);
+	}
+
+	/*
+	 * 5 GHz has one slot time and it is the short one, so set it here
+	 * rather than waiting for mac80211 to report an ERP slot change that
+	 * will never come. b43_set_slot_time() writes the shared-memory cell
+	 * and leaves the 2.4 GHz IFS slot register alone on this band.
+	 */
+	if (b43_current_band(dev->wl) == NL80211_BAND_5GHZ)
+		b43_short_slot_timing_enable(dev);
+
 	/* Disable sending probe responses from firmware.
 	 * Setting the MaxTime to one usec will always trigger
 	 * a timeout, so we never send any probe resp.
-- 
2.43.0
